{
  "id": "cloud-members-can-edit-my-records",
  "title": "Invite people who can change my records too: a role per owner, joined only through an invitation its owner made",
  "description": "A second person, or several, who can change everything you can: people who may all read AND change the same records (a partner, a co-organiser, a colleague, anyone you invite to edit with you). The group belongs to one person, its owner, and its role is named from that person's id (\"members-of-<id>\"). The owner's page creates an Invite holding only the invited address; the backend writes who made it into `owner`. Join Group, a cloud function the invited person's page calls, finds the invitations naming the caller's own address (from their session, not the request) and puts them in the role of the invitation's OWNER, never a group id the invitation carries: anyone signed in can create an Invite naming any group, and only `owner` is the server's word, so an invitation can only ever let someone into its creator's own group. Every record of the group is created by Add To Group, which runs as the system: it refuses a caller who is neither the owner nor in the role (the User node's `roles`, read from the caller's session), writes the GROUP owner into `owner` (a page could never name it), and stores access rules for the owner and the role ({\"<ownerId>\": read+write, \"role:members-of-<ownerId>\": read+write}). So members change a record straight from the page with Update Record (the role's write rule lets them), and the group's list is a Query Records filtered on `owner` = the group, which a record somebody else made and opened to the role can never match. Remove Member takes back the caller's own invitation and takes the member out of the caller's own group, whatever the invitation says. `createRole` is on in Join Group because the role name is built in code, one per owner, never typed. Declare Invite (withEmail, memberId) and Note (title) in provision_backend, both creator-owned (the default), and set Note's create rule to \"nobody\" (set_collection_permissions): a cloud function runs as the system and still creates, and a Note posted straight to the collection from a page is refused, so a check in Add To Group is not one a caller can walk around.",
  "demonstrates": [
    "NewDbModelProperties",
    "SetDbModelProperties",
    "DbCollection2",
    "CloudFunction2",
    "noodl.cloud.request",
    "net.noodl.user.User",
    "JavaScriptFunction",
    "noodl.cloud.addusertorole",
    "noodl.cloud.removeuserfromrole",
    "noodl.cloud.response"
  ],
